            <span style="flex:1">How do I avoid misunderstanding the scope or cost of a remodel?</span>
            <span class="faq-plus" style="flex:0 0 auto;font-family:Lato,sans-serif;font-size:22px;color:#7A3F96;transition:transform 200ms cubic-bezier(.25,.1,.25,1)">+</span>
          </summary>
          <p style="margin:0;padding:0 44px 22px 2px;font-size:15px;line-height:24px">Ask for clarity in writing before you commit. The CSLB says a home-improvement contract should describe the work and materials in detail, include a payment schedule, state who will secure permits, identify a completion date, and document approved changes through written change orders.</p>
        </details>
        <details style="border-bottom:1px solid #B5BCC0">
          <summary style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:18px;padding:20px 2px;cursor:pointer;font-family:'Noto Serif',Georgia,serif;font-size:18px;line-height:26px;color:#5D5D5D">
            <span style="flex:1">Do all general remodeling projects need permits?</span>
            <span class="faq-plus" style="flex:0 0 auto;font-family:Lato,sans-serif;font-size:22px;color:#7A3F96;transition:transform 200ms cubic-bezier(.25,.1,.25,1)">+</span>
          </summary>
          <p style="margin:0;padding:0 44px 22px 2px;font-size:15px;line-height:24px">It depends on the proposed work and the property’s jurisdiction. LA County lists remodels and renovations, along with new or expanded electrical, mechanical, and plumbing work, among work generally requiring permits.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
      <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;border-top:1px solid #B5BCC0">
